<Docs
  title="GitWarren and WSL — reviewing Linux repositories from the Windows app"
  description="A WSL distro is a host like any other. The Windows app lists your distributions, installs into the one you pick, and reviews its repositories as Linux paths."
  eyebrow="WSL"
  heading="A distro is a host, not a folder."
  intro="If your code lives inside a WSL distribution, that distribution is a machine. GitWarren treats it as one — it installs a daemon inside the distro and talks to it, rather than reaching across the boundary and reading Linux files as though they were Windows ones."
>
  <h2>Adding a distro</h2>

  <p>
    Open <strong>Other machines</strong> in the Windows app and add a host of
    kind <strong>WSL</strong>. The distributions on the machine are listed, you
    pick one, and GitWarren installs itself inside it over{" "}
    <code>wsl.exe</code> — the same installer that runs over ssh, because it was
    written against &ldquo;a machine with a shell and a <code>tar</code>&rdquo;
    rather than against ssh specifically.
  </p>

  <p>
    GitWarren runs as that distribution&#39;s own default user, so the files it
    reads have the ownership and the permissions they would have if you had
    opened a shell yourself.
  </p>

  <p>
    From there it is an ordinary host: add its repositories by their Linux
    paths, open reviews, point the agent that runs in that distro at the
    launcher inside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why not just open the Windows path?</h2>

  <p>
    Windows can see a distro&#39;s files at <code>\\wsl$\…</code>, and it is
    tempting to add one as an ordinary local repository. GitWarren{" "}
    <strong>refuses</strong> a WSL path offered as a local repository, on
    purpose.
  </p>

  <p>
    Reading a Linux repository through the Windows filesystem redirector gets
    you different line endings, different permissions, different case
    sensitivity, and a git that disagrees with the git inside the distro about
    what is modified. Worse, the agent working in that repo is a Linux process
    with Linux paths — a review keyed on Windows paths could not anchor a
    comment to anything it said.
  </p>

  <p>
    Installing into the distro costs one dialog and makes all of that go away:
    one git, one set of paths, one idea of what is dirty.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">Windows-native repositories stay first class</span>
    <p>
      None of this makes the Windows app worse for people who do not use WSL.
      Most Windows developers do not, and coding agents have run natively on
      Windows since late 2025. A repository on <code>C:\</code> is an ordinary
      local repository with no host in front of it. See <a
        href="/docs/windows">Running on Windows</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reaching a distro from somewhere else</h2>

  <p>
    <code>wsl.exe</code> only exists on the Windows machine itself. To review
    that distro&#39;s code from a Mac or a Linux laptop, reach it the way you
    would reach any other Linux box — over ssh, or over your tailnet if
    Tailscale runs inside the distro.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both work, and both are the same host from GitWarren&#39;s point of view; a
    distro added over ssh from your laptop and over <code>wsl.exe</code> from the
    Windows app is the same machine, with the same reviews, because the identity
    that settles it is the install rather than the route to it.
  </p>
</Docs>
